{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-80492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=2{x}^{2}.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/state.json" "file_sha256" "70dfc2d3901190177f89e5518ec1fd1e8ae9dad5f3a3be9f25ff404feb7c8814"} {"question" "q-80492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/assets/4fec059132b7bd02cb64f864b6982c0c64a66c22646334ca199fad12097ba435.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/state.json" "file_sha256" "70dfc2d3901190177f89e5518ec1fd1e8ae9dad5f3a3be9f25ff404feb7c8814"} {"question" "q-80492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054607/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6fc39514b7819b896c748a22fda886a33b909726f8e049309dbb73bddc29d37f"} {"question" "q-80492" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/